coming. Record lows have nearly stopped.","p_rec":"If St. Louis' climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, St. Louis has set <strong class=\"sig\">10 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 64 that chance alone would give, and <strong class=\"sig\">103 record highs</strong>, far more than the 64 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (58). St. Louis' all-time high is 115\u00b0F, on July 14, 1954.","t_chg":"St. Louis' nights have warmed since 1986. Its days have warmed.","p_chg":"Across 152 complete years: St. Louis' average low shows no clear change: +0.03\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.04 to +0.09) that includes zero. St. Louis' average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.18\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.12 to +0.24), about +2.7\u00b0F since 1874. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.44\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.06 to +0.81) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.69\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.24 to +1.15). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none. Highs rose in 10 of 12 months.","t_rain":"St. Louis has gotten wetter","p_rain":"St. Louis' yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.29 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.02 to +0.56), about +4.3 inches since 1874. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 41.7 inches a year. The wettest year was 2015 (61.24 in); the driest, 1953 (20.59 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 10 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}